Actually, let me think about the mathematics: Sturm's comparison theorem: u1'' + k1 u1 = 0, u2'' + k2 u2 = 0, k1 >= k2. If u1(a) = u2(a) = 0 and u2 has no zero in (a,b], then u1 has no zero in (a,b] — or contrapositively a zero of u1 in (a,b) implies a zero of u2. For k1 > k2, zeros of u1 interlace with zeros of u2: between two consecutive zeros of u2 there is a zero of u1 (when k1 >= k2 and k1 ≠ k2 somewhere).

So for k1 = 1 (sin) and k2 = 0 (t): zeros of u2 = t: only at 0. Between consecutive zeros of u2 (a=0, b=∞)... The engine requires u2 b = 0 (finite b). Since t has no positive zero, the two-curvature interlacing can't be instantiated with u2 = t. The natural "sharper" reading: u1 = sin has faster oscillation, so its first zero (π) comes before the "next zero" of u2 (which is at infinity). So indeed the literal statement "first positive zero of sin < π" is false, and the offered alternative is the correct route.

